----

== Phase 1: Data Loading ==

=== Step 1: Load Data ===

<code>control_total_geography.steps.load_data</code>

Reads each CSV listed under <code>data_tables</code> in settings (<code>control_target_xwalk</code>, <code>regional_geographies_xwalk</code>, <code>military_bases_xwalk</code>) from the <code>data/</code> folder into a <code>pandas.DataFrame</code> and saves it to the pipeline cache as a parquet file.

=== Step 2: Get Elmer Data ===

<code>control_total_geography.steps.get_elmer_data</code>

Connects to PSRC's '''ElmerGeo''' SQL Server database (VPN required) and, for every entry in the <code>ElmerGeo</code> settings list, reads the named feature class/columns, converts the configured <code>id_col</code> to <code>int64</code>, and saves the result as a geodataframe in the pipeline cache. (An analogous <code>Elmer</code> tabular loader also exists but is unused while the <code>Elmer</code> settings list is empty.)

----

== Phase 2: Control Geography Creation ==

=== Step 3: Create Control Geography ===

<code>control_total_geography.steps.create_control_geography</code>

Builds the <code>control</code> layer by preparing and unioning several polygon layers, each keyed by <code>control_id</code>:

# '''<code>prepare_psrc_region</code>''' — Filters <code>psrc_region</code> to in-region jurisdictions, joins to <code>regional_geographies_xwalk</code> on <code>county + juris</code> to get a <code>control_id</code>. Jurisdictions listed in <code>jurisdictions_to_explode</code> are split out, spatially joined to <code>old_control_areas</code> (<code>CONTROL18_DASHBOARD</code>), and dissolved back in so they can be split into multiple control areas instead of one.
# '''<code>prepare_military_bases</code>''' — Dissolves military base polygons by installation, joins to <code>military_bases_xwalk</code> to get <code>control_id</code>, and clips to the PSRC counties.
# '''<code>jblm_uga</code>''' — The JBLM UGA polygon is pulled out of the regional layer using the configured <code>jblm_uga_control_id</code> so it can be layered separately on top of the military-base union.
# '''<code>prepare_tribal_areas</code>''' — Clips <code>tribal_land</code> to the PSRC counties, keeps only the areas listed in <code>tribal_areas_keep</code>, and assigns their configured <code>control_id</code>.
# '''<code>prepare_natural_resource_areas</code>''' — Combines national forest, national park, and natural resource polygons, clips to PSRC counties, and (per county) merges small sliver polygons that fall within <code>nat_resource_sliver_buffer</code> feet of a resource polygon back into the resource area so they aren't left as tiny orphan control areas. Assigns <code>control_id</code> using <code>nat_resource_county_map</code>.

These layers are combined with repeated calls to <code>union_dissolve</code> (regional ∪ military → ∪ JBLM UGA → ∪ tribal → ∪ natural resource), which overlays two layers, fills missing IDs from the secondary layer, and dissolves by ID so the result has exactly one polygon per <code>control_id</code>. Finally, <code>control_target_xwalk</code> is merged in to attach <code>target_id</code>, <code>target_name</code>, <code>county_id</code>, and <code>exclude_from_target</code>.

Output: <code>control</code> geodataframe (columns: <code>control_id</code>, <code>control_name</code>, <code>target_id</code>, <code>target_name</code>, <code>county_id</code>, <code>exclude_from_target</code>, <code>geometry</code>).

----

== Phase 3: Target & Cities Geography ==

=== Step 4: Create Target Geography ===

<code>control_total_geography.steps.create_target_geography</code>

Dissolves the <code>control</code> layer by <code>target_id</code>/<code>target_name</code>, dropping the now-irrelevant <code>control_id</code>/<code>control_name</code>/<code>exclude_from_target</code> columns. Target areas group one or more control areas together and are the level at which county growth targets are later assigned in the downstream control-totals process.

Output: <code>target</code> geodataframe.

=== Step 5: Create Cities Geography ===

<code>control_total_geography.steps.create_cities_geography</code>

Filters <code>psrc_region</code> to features where <code>feat_type == 'city'</code>, sorts by county/jurisdiction, and assigns a sequential <code>city_id</code>. Also spatially joins current parcel points to the cities layer to build a <code>parcels_cities</code> crosswalk table (<code>parcel_id</code>, <code>city_id</code>, <code>juris</code>, <code>rgeo_class</code>, <code>cnty_fips</code>, <code>cnty_name</code>).

Outputs: <code>cities</code> geodataframe, <code>parcels_cities</code> table.

----

== Phase 4: Parcel HCT/TOD Flagging ==

=== Step 6: Flag HCT Parcels ===

<code>control_total_geography.steps.flag_hct_parcels</code>

For every current parcel point:

# '''<code>flag_rural_parcels</code>''' — Flags parcels that intersect the <code>rural</code> feature type in <code>psrc_region</code>.
# '''<code>flag_urban_center_parcels</code>''' — Flags parcels that intersect an <code>urban_centers</code> polygon.
# '''<code>flag_hct_parcels</code>''' — For each transit type in <code>hct_buffers</code> (<code>brt</code>, <code>commuter_r</code>, <code>light_rail</code>, <code>ferry</code>), buffers the non-rural <code>hct_stops</code> of that type by the configured distance and flags parcels that fall within the buffer.
# '''TOD code assignment''' — A single <code>tod</code> code is derived from the boolean flags, applied in priority order (later overrides earlier): default 0 → <code>urban_center</code> → 6 → <code>brt</code> → 1 → <code>ferry</code> → 5 → <code>commuter_r</code> → 2 → <code>light_rail</code> → 4 → <code>rural</code> → 0 (rural always wins).
# '''<code>assign_ids</code>''' — Spatially joins parcels to the <code>control</code> layer to get <code>control_id</code> (falling back to <code>sjoin_nearest</code> for any unmatched parcels), then derives <code>control_hct_id</code> = <code>control_id</code> + 1000 for parcels with a non-zero <code>tod</code> code, and sets <code>subreg_id</code> = <code>control_hct_id</code>.

{| class="wikitable"
! TOD Code !! Category
|-
| 0 || Non-HCT / rural (or none of the below)
|-
| 1 || BRT
|-
| 2 || Commuter rail
|-
| 4 || Light rail
|-
| 5 || Ferry
|-
| 6 || Urban center
|}

Outputs: <code>parcels_hct</code> (all flags + geometry), <code>hct_buffers</code> (buffered stop polygons), <code>parcels_control_hct</code> (tabular parcel/control/TOD crosswalk, no geometry).

----

== Phase 5: Control HCT Geography ==

=== Step 7: Save HCT Shapefile ===

<code>control_total_geography.steps.save_hct_shp</code>

Reads full parcel '''polygons''' directly from ElmerGeo (<code>PARCELS_URBANSIM_2023</code>, read straight from the database rather than the pipeline cache because the layer is too large to store efficiently), merges in each parcel's <code>control_id</code>/<code>control_hct_id</code> from <code>parcels_hct</code>, and dissolves by <code>(control_id, control_hct_id)</code> to build one polygon per control/HCT sub-area. Control names are merged back in and columns are renamed to <code>chct_id</code>/<code>chct_name</code>.

Output: <code>control_hct</code> geodataframe — the <code>control</code> layer split into HCT and non-HCT sub-areas.

----

== Phase 6: Output Export ==

=== Step 8: Write Outputs ===

<code>control_total_geography.steps.write_outputs</code>

For each layer in <code>output_layers</code> (<code>control</code>, <code>control_hct</code>, <code>target</code>, <code>cities</code>), reads the geodataframe from the pipeline cache and exports it to <code>output_geodatabase</code> (default <code>control.gdb</code>) in the configured <code>output_dir</code>, with the layer name suffixed by the last two digits of <code>control_areas_year</code> (e.g. <code>control26</code>). For each table in <code>output_tables</code> (<code>parcels_control_hct</code>, <code>parcels_cities</code>), exports the table to a CSV file of the same name.

----

